  <title>Export pictures</title>
  <p>Press <gui>Export</gui> (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>E</key></keyseq>) to make pictures from the scans. The scan is developed again every time, so you can export the same frame with different settings as often as you like.</p>
  <terms>
    <item><title><gui>Preset</gui></title><p>Settings for a purpose: <gui>Archive</gui>, <gui>Share</gui> or <gui>Print</gui>.</p></item>
    <item><title><gui>Format</gui></title><p>TIFF (8 or 16 bits, with or without compression), JPEG, PNG, or DNG. A DNG is the scan itself, undeveloped, for programs such as darktable and RawTherapee to develop. It carries the frame's rotation, the film speed and the details you entered as metadata, and opens cropped to the picture; the black around it is still in the file.</p></item>
    <item><title><gui>Size</gui></title><p>The original size, a long edge in pixels, or a percentage.</p></item>
    <item><title><gui>Colour Space</gui></title><p>sRGB, or Adobe RGB for printing.</p></item>
    <item><title><gui>Folder</gui> and <gui>File Name</gui></title><p>Where the pictures go, and what they are called.</p></item>
    <item><title><gui>Export After Each Scan</gui></title><p>Makes the picture as soon as a scan is saved.</p></item>
  </terms>
  <section id="names"><title>File names</title>
    <p>The file name is made from a template. <code>{roll}</code> is the name of the roll, <code>{frame}</code> the number of the frame (<code>{frame:03}</code> pads it with zeros to three digits), <code>{label}</code> the frame's label, or its number if it has none, <code>{film}</code> the film and <code>{date}</code> today's date. Anything else is copied as typed. A name that exists already gets (2), (3) and so on added: nothing is overwritten.</p>
  </section>
  <section id="queue"><title>The export queue</title>
    <p>Exports run one after another while you carry on working, and even while the scanner scans the next frame. The button with the arrow in the header bar lists them and can cancel one or show a finished picture in the file manager.</p>
  </section>
</page>
